        <abstract>
            <para>The data contain occurrence records of four native rainforest tree species and the mammals, birds, and reptiles detected at those trees, from a seed predation experiment on the Valparai Plateau, Western Ghats, Tamil Nadu, India (January–August 2020). The four focal tree species — Acronychia pedunculata, Cullenia exarillata, Ormosia travancorica, and Syzygium rubicundum — are mid-elevation tropical rainforest trees whose seeds are consumed by mammals to varying degrees. Individual fruiting trees were selected as focal trees in three rainforest fragments (Akkamalai-Iyerpadi, Andiparai, Iyerpadi-Top, Old-Valparai, Puthuthottam; area 30–100 ha) and in contiguous forest within the Anamalai Tiger Reserve. Camera traps placed at seed plots near and far from each focal tree recorded visiting mammals and birds (748 detections), and additional mammal and bird occurrences were recorded as ad-hoc observations (31 records). A total of 779 occurrence records are included, spanning 38 taxa: the four focal tree species (Plantae, Class Magnoliopsida) and 34 animal taxa (mammals, birds, reptiles). The data correspond to the following publication: Krishnan A, Osuri AM, Krishnadas M. 2022. Small mammals reduce distance dependence and increase seed predation risk in tropical rainforest fragments. Biotropica. 54:1428–1439. https://doi.org/10.1111/btp.13137</para>
        </abstract>
