        <methods>
            <methodStep>
                <description>
                    <para>Methods are as described in Krishnan et al. (2022). Individual fruiting trees of four native rainforest species (Acronychia pedunculata, Cullenia exarillata, Ormosia travancorica, Syzygium rubicundum) were selected as focal trees in contiguous forest and fragments. At each focal tree, seed plots consisting of 10 conspecific seeds in a 50 × 50 cm area were established &quot;near&quot; (within 3 m of the trunk) and &quot;far&quot; (20–30 m away). Camera traps were placed 2 m from each seed plot (30–60 cm above ground, video/photograph mode: three photographs at five-second intervals or a 30-second video) to identify mammal visitors. Cameras ran for up to 90 days or until all seeds were consumed. Mammal and bird occurrences encountered incidentally during fieldwork were additionally recorded as ad-hoc observations. Identification references: Prater (1948) and Menon (2003) for mammals; Gamble and Fischer (1935) and Pascal and Ramesh (1997) for plants.</para>
                </description>
            </methodStep>
            <sampling>
                <studyExtent>
                    <description>
                        <para>Focal trees of four rainforest species were selected in three rainforest fragments (total N = 32 focal trees) and contiguous forest within Anamalai Tiger Reserve (N = 30 focal trees) on the Valparai Plateau, Tamil Nadu. Fragments monitored: Akkamalai-Iyerpadi, Andiparai, Iyerpadi-Top, Old-Valparai, and Puthuthottam (area 30–100 ha). Camera traps were deployed at 16 locations in contiguous forest (676 trap nights) and 16 locations in fragments (317 trap nights). Fieldwork ran from January to August 2020.</para>
                    </description>
                </studyExtent>
                <samplingDescription>
                    <para>This dataset contains two types of occurrence records. (1) Tree occurrences: each focal tree individual of the four study species (Acronychia pedunculata, Cullenia exarillata, Ormosia travancorica, Syzygium rubicundum) is represented as a plant occurrence record at its mapped location, forming the spatial anchor for associated animal records. (2) Animal occurrences: mammals and birds detected by camera trap at seed plots near each focal tree (basisOfRecord: MachineObservation; samplingProtocol: camera trap detection) or encountered incidentally during field visits (basisOfRecord: HumanObservation; samplingProtocol: ad-hoc observation). Animal detection events are linked to their focal tree via the eventID. The number of detections per visit and behavioural activity are recorded in occurrenceRemarks (e.g. &quot;n_detections:1|activity:Passing&quot;).</para>
                </samplingDescription>
            </sampling>
            <qualityControl>
                <description>
                    <para>Camera trap images and videos were reviewed and species identified by Aparna Krishnan using standard field references. Some records were identified only to genus, family, or order where image quality did not permit species-level identification; original identifications as recorded are retained in verbatimIdentification. Focal tree locations were recorded with a GPS (coordinateUncertaintyInMeters = 10 m). GBIF-reconciled scientific names are provided alongside original field identifications.</para>
                </description>
            </qualityControl>
        </methods>
        <project id="ncf-ncbs-arp-seedpredation">
            <title>Effects of Forest Fragmentation on Mammal-mediated Seed Predation in the Western Ghats, India</title>
            <personnel>
                <individualName>
                    <givenName>Aparna</givenName>
                    <surName>Krishnan</surName>
                </individualName>
                <userId directory="https://orcid.org/">0000-0001-9781-1157</userId>
                <role>originator</role>
            </personnel>
            <abstract>
                <para>This project examined how forest fragmentation affects seed predation by mammals and the distance dependence of seed predation risk in tropical rainforest fragments on the Valparai Plateau, Western Ghats, India. A field experiment was conducted at individual fruiting trees of four native rainforest species (Acronychia pedunculata, Cullenia exarillata, Ormosia travancorica, Syzygium rubicundum) in contiguous forest (Anamalai Tiger Reserve) and rainforest fragments. Camera traps placed at seed plots near and far from each focal tree recorded visiting mammal species. The study found markedly higher mammal-mediated seed predation in fragments, with reduced distance dependence for tree species whose seeds are primarily consumed by mammals.</para>
            </abstract>
